            "typeName": "dsDescription",
            "multiple": true,
            "typeClass": "compound",
            "value": [
              {
                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "We investigate firms’ decisions to pay elective stock dividends, known in\r\nthe UK as scrip dividends. Scrip dividends give investors the choice\r\nbetween receiving new shares or the equivalent value as a cash dividend.\r\nUK firms paying scrip dividends are more likely to be financially\r\nconstrained, and scrip dividends are used more when access to external\r\nfinancing is costly. Our results are robust to using the 2008 financial crisis\r\nas an exogenous shock to credit supply. Cash preservation is the most\r\nimportant corporate incentive to use scrip dividends as they tend to be\r\ndistributed in combination with dividend cuts and with major corporate\r\ninvestments such as debt-financed mergers and acquisitions. Analysis of\r\nUS dividend reinvestment plans by which investors purchase new shares\r\nconfirms firms’ cash-preservation motives"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
